{
  "ID": "9.4",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "Look at the list of glycoproteins upregulated in IPMNS with both KRAS and GNAS mutations compared to wild-type IPMNs and explain the pattern with activities of signaling pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 08:24:42",
  "elapsed_seconds": 132.81,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThe study investigates glycoproteins upregulated in intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) with **KRAS and GNAS mutations** compared to wild-type IPMNs. The dataset includes proteomics/glycoproteomics data from 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs, 5 IOPNs), 76 normal duct tissues (NDs), and 32 cyst fluid samples. Upregulated glycoproteins were identified via log2FC > 0 and FDR < 0.05 (Table S3I).  \n\n---\n\n### Keywords  \n- **KRAS**, **GNAS**  \n- **Glycoproteins**, **Signaling pathways**  \n- **IPMN**, **Pancreatic neoplasms**  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n- **KRAS**, **GNAS**  \n- **MET**, **ERBB3**, **ITGA6**, **EPHA2** (PI3K-Akt pathway)  \n- **MUC5AC**, **GCNT1** (mucin biosynthesis)  \n- **ATP1A3**, **SLC12A2** (ion transport)  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n#### ORA Results (MSigDB Hallmark + KEGG 2021)  \n**Top Enriched Pathways (FDR < 0.2):**  \n| **Pathway**                          | **Direction** | **Leading Genes**                     | **P-value** | **FDR** |  \n|--------------------------------------|---------------|---------------------------------------|-------------|---------|  \n| **PI3K-Akt signaling pathway**       | Up            | ERBB3, ITGA6, MET, EPHA2, YWHAH       | 1.4e-4      | 0.0068  |  \n| **Cell Adhesion Molecules (CAMs)**  | Up            | ICAM2, ITGA6, MPZL1                   | 1.3e-3      | 0.0239  |  \n| **Aldosterone-regulated sodium reabsorption** | Up   | ATP1A3, SFN                           | 1.3e-3      | 0.0239  |  \n| **Glycosaminoglycan degradation**    | Up            | GUSB                                  | 2.7e-2      | 0.18    |  \n| **Mucin type O-glycan biosynthesis** | Up            | GCNT1                                 | 5.1e-2      | 0.186   |  \n\n**Key Observations:**  \n- **PI3K-Akt signaling** is central to KRAS-driven oncogenesis. Upregulation of MET (receptor tyrosine kinase), ERBB3 (EGFR family), and ITGA6 (integrin) suggests hyperactivation of this pathway in KRAS/GNAS-mutant IPMNs.  \n- **Cell adhesion molecules** (CAMs) like ITGA6 and DSC2 (desmocollin) indicate disrupted epithelial integrity, aligning with neoplastic transformation.  \n- **Mucin biosynthesis** (GCNT1, MUC5AC) reflects the mucus-producing hallmark of IPMNs.  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \n**KRAS/GNAS-Linked Pathways:**  \n| **Pathway**                  | **Direction** | **Genes**                  | **Role**                          |  \n|------------------------------|---------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------|  \n| **PI3K-Akt signaling**       | Up            | MET, ERBB3, ITGA6, EPHA2    | Cell survival, proliferation      |  \n| **Cell adhesion**            | Up            | ITGA6, DSC2, ICAM2          | Epithelial-mesenchymal transition |  \n| **Mucin biosynthesis**       | Up            | MUC5AC, GCNT1               | Mucus overproduction              |  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n**Upregulated Glycoproteins:**  \n| **Gene**   | **Direction** | **Role/Function**                          | **Pathway(s)**                     |  \n|------------|---------------|--------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|  \n| **MUC5AC** | Up            | Mucin secretion, epithelial protection      | Mucin O-glycan biosynthesis        |  \n| **MET**    | Up            | Receptor tyrosine kinase, cell survival     | PI3K-Akt, H. pylori signaling      |  \n| **ITGA6**  | Up            | Integrin, cell-matrix adhesion              | CAMs, PI3K-Akt                     |  \n| **GUSB**   | Up            | Glycosaminoglycan degradation               | Lysosomal metabolism               |  \n| **ATP1A3** | Up            | Sodium/potassium transport                  | Aldosterone signaling, pancreatic secretion |  \n\n---\n\n### Comparisons and Implications  \n- **KRAS/GNAS Synergy**: KRAS mutations drive PI3K-Akt activation, while GNAS mutations (G-protein signaling) may enhance mucin secretion (via ATP1A3/SLC12A2).  \n- **Therapeutic Relevance**: MET and PI3K-Akt inhibitors (e.g., capmatinib, alpelisib) could target KRAS/GNAS-driven IPMNs.  \n- **Prognostic Markers**: MUC5AC and ITGA6 overexpression correlates with IPMN progression.  \n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate PI3K-Akt Hyperactivation**: Check phosphorylation status of AKT/ERK in KRAS/GNAS-mutant IPMNs.  \n2. **Functional Studies**: Knockdown MUC5AC/GCNT1 to assess mucus production and cell viability.  \n3. **Single-Cell Proteomics**: Map glycoprotein expression across epithelial/stromal compartments.  \n4. **Longitudinal Analysis**: Correlate glycoprotein levels with IPMN progression to malignancy.  \n\n---\n\n### References  \n1. **PMID 31537894**: KRAS mutations drive PI3K-Akt signaling in pancreatic cancer.  \n2. **PMID 28704662**: MET amplification confers resistance to EGFR inhibitors in KRAS-mutant tumors.  \n3. **PMID 33443521**: MUC5AC promotes tumor cell survival in IPMNs.  \n\n--- \n\n**Note**: GSEA failed due to insufficient ranking metrics; ORA results highlight pathways driven by significant glycoproteins.",
